[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 4:51] Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years. Separately, you said the per-cup price rose about 10% on its own, so volume must be falling. Walk me through that, given what we know about menu prices and what's in the average ticket. Before we wrap, on what you asked about earlier: Average revenue per store is $2.4M a year.

[candidate · 6:21] Let me walk it. Menu prices are flat, so the only way the per-cup price gets to $6.80 from $6.20 is mix — customers trading up to pricier drinks. So effectively a 10% realized price increase per cup without a menu change.

Now, revenue grew 15% and the footprint is flat at 200 stores, so all of it is same-store. If price per cup is up 10% and total revenue is up 15%, cups are only up about 5% — 1.15 divided by 1.10 is 1.045. So volume is nearly flat while the ticket carries the growth.

Sizing the bean hit: 200 stores at $2.4M is $480M revenue. Beans were 25% of COGS, and applying that 25% share to today's 58% COGS line gives 14.5% of revenue, or about $70M on beans. At 40% inflation, that's roughly $20M of added cost — about 4 points of margin from beans alone.
