[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 7:57] Understood. Exhibit A shows the cost structure over time. Looking at it alongside your numbers, what does it tell you, and what's left of the 16-point COGS increase that your input inflation doesn't explain?

[candidate · 9:27] The exhibit says this is steady, not a shock — 42 to 50 to 58, eight points a year, each year. That's chronic, which fits input inflation running continuously rather than one bad harvest.

On the gap: my inflation math explains about 13.7 of the 16 points, so roughly 2.3 points are unexplained — about $11M on the $480M base. That's real money. Candidates would be waste and spoilage, shrink, portion control drifting as baristas handle more items per ticket, or supplier terms worsening.

One thing I'd flag: overhead went 12 to 14 points. If I take the 40% increase we saw on beans and apply it to that overhead line, 12% × 1.40 = 16.8% — we'd expect worse than 14%, so overhead is actually being managed reasonably well relative to input trends.

And labor holding at exactly 22% while revenue grew 15% means labor dollars grew 15% too — so they haven't gained any leverage from the bigger ticket.
