[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 9:29] Walk me through the overhead comparison. Why would the bean cost increase apply to the overhead line in the first place?

[candidate · 10:59] It wouldn't, honestly. That was sloppy — the 40% is raw coffee bean inflation, which flows through COGS. Overhead is rent, utilities, corporate, marketing; there's no mechanism by which bean prices drive it. I was pattern-matching a number I had onto a line it doesn't belong to.

The right read on overhead is simpler: it went from 12% to 14% of revenue while revenue itself grew 15%. So overhead dollars grew from 12% × $417M ≈ $50M to 14% × $480M ≈ $67M — about 34% growth in absolute terms, well ahead of the 15% revenue growth. That's not well managed, it's the opposite. Something in there is growing faster than the business.

I'd want the overhead split before concluding, but as it stands it's 2 points of the 18, versus 16 from COGS. Still a secondary issue.
