[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 11:02] Okay. Beyond what we've discussed, what else could the client do to reverse the margin decline?

[candidate · 12:32] A few levers beyond cost control.

First, pricing. Menu prices haven't moved in two years while inputs rose 40%. That's the biggest untapped lever — and the ticket data suggests headroom, since customers already absorbed a move from $6.20 to $6.80 per cup without resisting. A 5% menu increase on $480M is $24M straight to the bottom line, roughly 5 points of margin, if volume holds.

Second, procurement: lock in forward contracts or hedge beans, consolidate suppliers, renegotiate on the strength of 200 stores.

Third, menu engineering: push the higher-margin items customers are already trading up into, and trim low-margin SKUs with volatile inputs — dairy especially, since other inputs rose 37.5% and are the larger dollar block.

Fourth, waste: that unexplained ~2.3 points is addressable with portion standards and spoilage tracking.

Fifth, labor's flat at 22% but got no leverage from growth — scheduling against traffic could free a point.
